(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Probability & Statistics"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Probability & Statistics"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Probability and Statistics for Engineers: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nState each event or variable clearly, name the distribution you use and its parameters, and give a numerical answer with an interpretation. All numerical answers below were computed and checked with software (Python, SciPy). Use tables or a calculator for normal and t values.\n\n# Assignment 1: Probability and Discrete Random Variables (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Basic probability (10 marks)** (a) Two cards are drawn without replacement from a 52-card deck. Find the probability that both are aces [2]. (b) In a group of 23 people, find the probability that at least two share a birthday (365 equally likely days) [3]. (c) Find the probability of a five-card full house (total hands C(52,5) = 2,598,960) [3]. (d) Find the probability of winning a 6/49 lottery jackpot [2].\n\n**Q2. Conditional probability and Bayes (12 marks)** (a) A disease affects 1 percent of a population. A test is positive with probability 0.95 for a sick person and 0.10 for a healthy person. Find P(sick | positive) and explain the result [4]. (b) Factory A makes 50 percent of the items with 2 percent defective, B makes 30 percent with 3 percent defective, C makes 20 percent with 5 percent defective. Find P(defective) and P(made by A | defective) [4]. (c) Monty Hall: a prize is behind one of three doors; you choose one, the host opens a goat door among the other two, then offers a switch. Find the probability of winning by switching [4].\n\n**Q3. Reliability (8 marks)** Three independent components have reliabilities 0.9, 0.8 and 0.95. Find the reliability if they are (a) in series [2], (b) in parallel [2], (c) arranged as component 1 in series with the parallel pair of components 2 and 3 [4].\n\n**Q4. Discrete random variables (10 marks)** (a) A random variable X takes the values 0, 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.1, 0.3, 0.4, 0.2. Find E[X], Var(X), P(X ≥ 2) and the CDF [5]. (b) Let S be the sum of two fair dice. Find P(S = 7), E[S] and Var(S) [3]. (c) Find E[3X - 2] and Var(3X - 2) for the X of part (a) [2].\n\n**Q5. Standard discrete distributions (14 marks)** (a) X ~ Binomial(10, 0.3): find P(X = 3), P(X ≤ 2), E[X] and Var(X) [4]. (b) X ~ Poisson(4): find P(X = 2) and P(X ≥ 1) [3]. (c) A bit is flipped with probability p = 0.2 each time, independently: find the probability that the first success is on the 5th trial and the expected number of trials [3]. (d) A packet of 1000 bits has a bit error probability 10^-3 per bit. Find the probability of no error exactly and with the Poisson approximation [4].\n\n**Q6. Expectation techniques (8 marks)** (a) Using indicator variables, find the expected number of fixed points of a random permutation of n objects [3]. (b) How many throws of a fair die are needed on average to see all six faces? [3] (c) Find E[X] for a game that pays $10 with probability 0.1, $2 with probability 0.4 and loses $5 otherwise [2].\n\n**Q7. Inequalities (8 marks)** (a) A non-negative variable has mean 50: bound P(X ≥ 100) with Markov's inequality [2]. (b) A variable has mean 50 and standard deviation 5: bound P(|X - 50| ≥ 15) with Chebyshev [2]. (c) Compare the Chebyshev bound with the exact value if X is normal [2]. (d) Why are these bounds useful although they are loose? [2]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) P = (4/52)(3/51) = 12/2652 = 1/221 = **0.00452**.\n(b) P(no match) = Π_{i=0}^{22} (365 - i)/365 = 0.4927, so **P(match) = 0.507**: more likely than not with just 23 people.\n(c) Choose the rank of the triple (13), its suits C(4,3) = 4, the rank of the pair (12) and its suits C(4,2) = 6: 13 × 4 × 12 × 6 = 3744. P = 3744/2,598,960 = **0.00144** (about 1 in 694).\n(d) 1/C(49, 6) = 1/13,983,816 = **7.15 × 10^-8**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: P(+) = 0.95 × 0.01 + 0.10 × 0.99 = 0.0095 + 0.099 = 0.1085.\nStep 2: P(sick | +) = 0.0095/0.1085 = **0.0876 (8.8 percent)**.\nStep 3: Although the test is \"95 percent sensitive\", most positives are false because the disease is rare (many healthy people × a 10 percent false-positive rate): the base-rate effect.\nStep 4: Repeating the test or using a more specific test is needed.\n(b) P(defective) = 0.5(0.02) + 0.3(0.03) + 0.2(0.05) = 0.01 + 0.009 + 0.01 = **0.029**. P(A | defective) = 0.01/0.029 = **0.345**.\n(c) Your first choice is right with probability 1/3. The host's action (always revealing a goat) gives no information about your door, so staying wins with 1/3 and **switching wins with 2/3**. (Enumerating the three equally likely prize positions shows switching wins in two of them.)\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Series: 0.9 × 0.8 × 0.95 = **0.684**.\n(b) Parallel (at least one works): 1 - (0.1)(0.2)(0.05) = **0.999**.\n(c) The parallel pair works with probability 1 - (0.2)(0.05) = 0.99; in series with component 1: 0.9 × 0.99 = **0.891**.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: E[X] = 0(0.1) + 1(0.3) + 2(0.4) + 3(0.2) = **1.7**.\nStep 2: E[X²] = 0.3 + 1.6 + 1.8 = 3.7, so Var = 3.7 - 2.89 = **0.81** (σ = 0.9).\nStep 3: P(X ≥ 2) = 0.4 + 0.2 = **0.6**.\nStep 4: CDF: F(0) = 0.1, F(1) = 0.4, F(2) = 0.8, F(3) = 1.\n(b) 6 of the 36 outcomes give 7: **P = 1/6**. E[S] = 3.5 + 3.5 = **7**; the dice are independent, so Var(S) = 2 × 35/12 = **35/6 = 5.83**.\n(c) E[3X - 2] = 3(1.7) - 2 = **3.1**; Var(3X - 2) = 9 Var(X) = **7.29** (the constant does not change the variance).\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: P(X = 3) = C(10,3)(0.3)^3(0.7)^7 = 120 × 0.027 × 0.0823543 = **0.2668**.\nStep 2: P(X ≤ 2) = 0.0282 + 0.1211 + 0.2335 = **0.3828**.\nStep 3: E = np = **3**; Var = np(1 - p) = **2.1**.\n(b) P(X = 2) = e^-4 4²/2! = 0.01832 × 8 = **0.1465**. P(X ≥ 1) = 1 - e^-4 = **0.9817**.\n(c) P(first success on trial 5) = (0.8)^4 (0.2) = **0.0819**; E[trials] = 1/p = **5**.\n(d) Step 1: Exactly: (1 - 10^-3)^1000 = **0.3677**.\nStep 2: Poisson with λ = np = 1: e^-1 = **0.3679** (very close, since n is large and p is small).\nStep 3: The expected number of errors is 1.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Let I_k = 1 if element k is in its own position. P(I_k = 1) = 1/n, so by linearity E[number of fixed points] = n × (1/n) = **1** (for every n), even though the I_k are dependent.\n(b) Waiting for the k-th new face when k - 1 have been seen takes on average 6/(7 - k) throws: 6/6 + 6/5 + 6/4 + 6/3 + 6/2 + 6/1 = 6(1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/6) = **14.7 throws**.\n(c) The loss probability is 1 - 0.1 - 0.4 = 0.5: E[X] = 10(0.1) + 2(0.4) - 5(0.5) = 1 + 0.8 - 2.5 = **-0.7 dollars** (the game is unfavourable).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) P(X ≥ 100) ≤ 50/100 = **0.5**.\n(b) k = 15/5 = 3, so P ≤ 1/9 = **0.111**.\n(c) For a normal variable P(|Z| ≥ 3) = **0.0027**, so the bound is about 40 times too large.\n(d) They hold for **any** distribution with the given mean (and variance), so they give safe worst-case guarantees when the distribution is unknown (and Markov/Chebyshev are used in proofs of the law of large numbers).\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Continuous Distributions, Joint Distributions and the CLT (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Probability density functions (12 marks)** A density is f(x) = c x² for 0 ≤ x ≤ 3 and 0 otherwise. (a) Find c [2]. (b) Find P(1 ≤ X ≤ 2) [2]. (c) Find the CDF [2]. (d) Find E[X] and Var(X) [4]. (e) Find the median [2].\n\n**Q2. Uniform and exponential (10 marks)** (a) A router's packet arrival time is uniform on (0, 10) ms: find P(X > 7) and E, Var [3]. (b) A device lifetime is exponential with mean 2 hours: find P(T > 3), the median lifetime and P(T > 5 | T > 2) [4]. (c) A series system has three independent exponential components with failure rates 0.01, 0.02 and 0.03 per hour: find the system failure rate, MTTF and reliability at 10 h [3].\n\n**Q3. The normal distribution (14 marks)** X ~ N(100, 15²). (a) Find P(X > 130) [2]. (b) Find P(85 < X < 115) [2]. (c) Find the 95th percentile [2]. (d) The lengths of two parts are independent N(5, 2²) and N(3, 1.5²): find the distribution of the difference X - Y and P(X > Y) [4]. (e) A voltage is N(0, 1) volts; find the probability that its absolute value exceeds 2 and 3 volts [2]. (f) Explain standardisation and the 68-95-99.7 rule [2].\n\n**Q4. Joint distributions (14 marks)** X and Y have the joint pmf p(0,0) = 0.1, p(0,1) = 0.2, p(0,2) = 0.1, p(1,0) = 0.2, p(1,1) = 0.3, p(1,2) = 0.1. (a) Find the marginals [3]. (b) Are X and Y independent? [2] (c) Find E[X], E[Y], E[XY], Cov(X, Y) and the correlation [6]. (d) Find P(Y = 1 | X = 1) and E[Y | X = 1] [3].\n\n**Q5. Law of large numbers and CLT (12 marks)** (a) A population has mean 50 and standard deviation 12. For samples of size 36 find P(X̄ > 53) [3]. (b) X ~ Binomial(100, 0.5): approximate P(X ≥ 60) with the normal distribution and the continuity correction, and compare with the exact value 0.0284 [4]. (c) How large must n be so that the standard error of a mean with σ = 12 is at most 1? [2] (d) State the CLT and why it justifies Gaussian noise models [3].\n\n**Q6. Transformations and processes (8 marks)** (a) X ~ Uniform(0, 1) and Y = X²: find f_Y and E[Y] [3]. (b) Calls arrive as a Poisson process with rate 3 per minute: find P(no calls in a minute) and the probability that the next call takes more than 30 s [3]. (c) State two properties of the Poisson process [2].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) ∫(0 to 3) c x² dx = c (27/3) = 9c = 1, so **c = 1/9**.\n(b) P = ∫(1 to 2) x²/9 dx = (8 - 1)/27 = **7/27 = 0.259**.\n(c) F(x) = ∫(0 to x) t²/9 dt = **x³/27** for 0 ≤ x ≤ 3 (0 below, 1 above).\n(d) Step 1: E[X] = ∫ x · x²/9 dx = (1/9)(81/4) = **2.25**.\nStep 2: E[X²] = ∫ x⁴/9 dx = (1/9)(243/5) = 5.4.\nStep 3: Var = 5.4 - 5.0625 = **0.3375**.\n(e) F(m) = 1/2: m³ = 13.5, **m = 2.381**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) P(X > 7) = 3/10 = **0.3**; E = 5 ms; Var = (b - a)²/12 = 100/12 = **8.33** ms².\n(b) Step 1: The rate is λ = 0.5: P(T > 3) = e^(-1.5) = **0.223**.\nStep 2: Median: 1 - e^(-0.5 t) = 0.5, t = 2 ln 2 = **1.386 h**.\nStep 3: Memoryless: P(T > 5 | T > 2) = P(T > 3) = **0.223**.\n(c) The system fails when any component fails: the rates add, **λ_sys = 0.06/h**; MTTF = 1/0.06 = **16.7 h**; R(10) = e^(-0.6) = **0.549**.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Z = (130 - 100)/15 = 2: P = **0.0228**.\n(b) z = ±1: P = **0.6827**.\n(c) 100 + 1.645 × 15 = **124.7**.\n(d) D = X - Y is normal with mean 5 - 3 = 2 and variance 4 + 2.25 = 6.25 (variances add for the difference of independent variables): **D ~ N(2, 2.5²)**. P(X > Y) = P(D > 0) = Φ(2/2.5) = Φ(0.8) = **0.788**.\n(e) P(|Z| > 2) = **0.0455**; P(|Z| > 3) = **0.0027**.\n(f) Standardisation Z = (X - μ)/σ turns any normal variable into N(0, 1), so one table serves all normal distributions. About 68 percent of values lie within 1σ of the mean, 95 percent within 2σ and 99.7 percent within 3σ.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) P(X = 0) = 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.1 = **0.4**; P(X = 1) = **0.6**. P(Y = 0) = 0.3, P(Y = 1) = 0.5, P(Y = 2) = **0.2** (sum 1).\n(b) If independent, p(0,0) would equal P(X = 0) P(Y = 0) = 0.4 × 0.3 = 0.12, but it is 0.1: **not independent**.\n(c) E[X] = **0.6**; E[Y] = 0.5 + 0.4 = **0.9**; E[XY] = 1×1×0.3 + 1×2×0.1 = **0.5**; Cov = 0.5 - 0.6 × 0.9 = **-0.04**. Var(X) = 0.6 × 0.4 = 0.24; E[Y²] = 0.5 + 0.8 = 1.3 so Var(Y) = 1.3 - 0.81 = 0.49. ρ = -0.04/sqrt(0.24 × 0.49) = **-0.117** (very weak negative correlation).\n(d) P(Y = 1 | X = 1) = 0.3/0.6 = **0.5**. The conditional pmf of Y given X = 1 is (1/3, 1/2, 1/6) for y = 0, 1, 2, so E[Y | X = 1] = 0.5 + 1/3 = **0.833**.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) The standard error is 12/sqrt 36 = 2, so P(X̄ > 53) = P(Z > 1.5) = **0.0668**.\n(b) Step 1: For Binomial(100, 0.5): μ = 50, σ = 5.\nStep 2: P(X ≥ 60) = P(X > 59.5) ≈ P(Z > 1.9) = **0.0287**.\nStep 3: The exact value is 0.0284: an excellent approximation.\n(c) σ/sqrt n ≤ 1 gives n ≥ 144.\n(d) **CLT:** the standardised mean of n i.i.d. variables with finite variance tends to N(0, 1) as n → ∞. Noise in electronic systems is the sum of many tiny independent contributions (thermal motion of electrons), so it is well modelled as Gaussian regardless of the distribution of each contribution.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) For 0 < y < 1: F_Y(y) = P(X² ≤ y) = P(X ≤ sqrt y) = sqrt y, so **f_Y(y) = 1/(2 sqrt y)** and E[Y] = ∫ y/(2 sqrt y) dy = **1/3** (the same as E[X²] = ∫ x² dx).\n(b) P(no call in 1 minute) = e^(-3) = **0.0498**. The time to the next call is Exponential(3 per minute): P(T > 0.5 min) = e^(-1.5) = **0.223**.\n(c) Increments over disjoint intervals are **independent**; the number of events in an interval of length t is **Poisson(λt)**; inter-arrival times are independent Exponential(λ) (memoryless).\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Statistics, Inference, Regression and Markov Chains (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Descriptive statistics (10 marks)** Data: 12, 15, 11, 18, 14, 15, 13, 40, 16, 14. Find the mean, median, mode(s), sample variance and standard deviation, quartiles (linear interpolation), the IQR and the outlier fences. Identify any outlier and discuss which summary statistics you would report.\n\n**Q2. Estimation (10 marks)** (a) Derive the maximum likelihood estimator of λ for n independent Exponential(λ) observations and compute it for the data 1.2, 0.8, 3.5, 2.1, 4.9 [4]. (b) Show that the MLE of the Bernoulli parameter p is the sample proportion [3]. (c) Explain why the sample variance uses n - 1 and compute both versions for the data of (a) [3].\n\n**Q3. Confidence intervals (12 marks)** (a) n = 25, x̄ = 52, s = 10 (normal data): find the 95 percent confidence interval for μ [3]. (b) The same x̄ with σ = 10 known [2]. (c) 230 of 400 people prefer a design: find a 95 percent confidence interval for the proportion [3]. (d) How many people are needed for a margin of error of 3 percentage points at 95 percent confidence? [2] (e) Interpret a confidence interval correctly [2].\n\n**Q4. Hypothesis tests (18 marks)** (a) H0: μ = 100, H1: μ ≠ 100, n = 49, x̄ = 103, σ = 14: z-test [4]. (b) n = 16, x̄ = 52.5, s = 6, H0: μ = 50: t-test [4]. (c) Paired data (before minus after) 2, 1, 3, 0, 2, 4, 1, 3: test whether the mean difference is zero [4]. (d) A die is rolled 60 times with face counts 8, 12, 9, 11, 13, 7: chi-square goodness of fit [3]. (e) A one-sided z-test (α = 0.05, σ = 10, n = 25) of H0: μ = 100 against H1: μ > 100 when the true mean is 104: find β and the power [3].\n\n**Q5. Regression (10 marks)** x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; y = 2.1, 3.9, 6.2, 7.8, 10.1. (a) Compute the least-squares line [4]. (b) Compute r and R² [3]. (c) Predict y at x = 6 and comment on extrapolation [3].\n\n**Q6. Markov chains (14 marks)** (a) Weather: sunny → sunny 0.8, sunny → rainy 0.2, rainy → sunny 0.4, rainy → rainy 0.6. Find the two-step transition matrix, the probability of rain two days after a sunny day, the stationary distribution and the mean number of days between rainy days (mean return time) [7]. (b) A three-state chain has P = [[0.5, 0.5, 0], [0.25, 0.5, 0.25], [0, 0.5, 0.5]]: find the stationary distribution [3]. (c) A gambler with 2 units bets 1 unit at a time on a coin with winning probability 0.4 and stops at 0 or 4 units: find the probability of reaching 4 [4].\n\n**Q7. Applications (6 marks)** (a) A link has a 10^-3 bit error rate: find the expected number of errors in a 1000-bit packet and the probability that a packet is error-free [2]. (b) An M/M/1 server has arrival rate 8 and service rate 10 per second: find ρ, L and W [2]. (c) A Beta(2, 2) prior is updated with 7 successes in 10 trials: give the posterior and its mean [2].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.** Step 1: n = 10.\nStep 2: **Mean** = 168/10 = **16.8**.\nStep 3: Sorted: 11, 12, 13, 14, 14, 15, 15, 16, 18, 40: **median** = (14 + 15)/2 = **14.5**.\nStep 4: **Mode:** **14 and 15** (both occur twice: bimodal).\nStep 5: **Variance** s² = Σ(x - 16.8)²/9 = 633.6/9 = **70.4**, **s = 8.39**.\nStep 6: Quartiles (linear interpolation): **Q1 = 13.25, Q3 = 15.75**, **IQR = 2.5**.\nStep 7: Fences: 13.25 - 3.75 = **9.5** and 15.75 + 3.75 = **19.5**: the value **40 is an outlier** (it is far above 19.5).\nStep 8: The outlier inflates the mean (without 40 the mean is 14.2) and the standard deviation (without it s = 2.1).\nStep 9: **Report the median and IQR** (robust statistics), and mention the outlier (check for a measurement error before removing it).\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) L(λ) = Π λ e^(-λ x_i) = λ^n e^(-λ Σx). ln L = n ln λ - λ Σx; d/dλ = n/λ - Σx = 0, so **λ̂ = n/Σx = 1/x̄**. The data sum to 12.5, x̄ = 2.5, so **λ̂ = 0.4** (a mean life of 2.5 units).\n(b) For Bernoulli data with s successes: ln L = s ln p + (n - s) ln(1 - p); setting the derivative to zero gives s/p = (n - s)/(1 - p), so **p̂ = s/n**, the sample proportion.\n(c) Σ(x_i - x̄)² has expectation (n - 1)σ² because the deviations are measured from x̄ (fitted from the same data), which makes them smaller than deviations from μ by the amount n(x̄ - μ)². Dividing by n - 1 gives an **unbiased** estimator. For the data: Σ(x - 2.5)² = 1.69 + 2.89 + 1.00 + 0.16 + 5.76 = 11.5, so s² = 11.5/4 = **2.875** (unbiased) and the divisor-n version is 11.5/5 = **2.3** (the MLE of the normal variance is biased low by the factor (n - 1)/n).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) SE = 10/5 = 2, t_(24, 0.025) = 2.064: 52 ± 4.13: **(47.87, 56.13)**.\n(b) z = 1.96: 52 ± 3.92 = **(48.08, 55.92)** (narrower, because σ is known).\n(c) p̂ = 230/400 = 0.575, SE = sqrt(0.575 × 0.425/400) = 0.0247: 0.575 ± 1.96 × 0.0247 = **(0.527, 0.623)**.\n(d) n = z² p(1 - p)/E² = 1.96² × 0.25/0.03² = 1067.1, so **n = 1068** (using the worst case p = 0.5).\n(e) If the procedure is repeated on many samples, **95 percent of the intervals so constructed contain the true parameter**. For one computed interval the parameter is either inside or outside; the 95 percent describes the method, not this interval.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) z = (103 - 100)/(14/7) = **1.5**; two-sided p = 2 × 0.0668 = **0.134** > 0.05: **do not reject H0** (insufficient evidence of a difference).\n(b) t = (52.5 - 50)/(6/4) = **1.667** with 15 degrees of freedom; the critical value is 2.131 and p = **0.116**: **do not reject H0**.\n(c) Differences d = 2, 1, 3, 0, 2, 4, 1, 3: mean = 2, s = 1.309, SE = 1.309/sqrt 8 = 0.463, **t = 4.32** with 7 degrees of freedom (critical 2.365), p = **0.0035**: **reject H0**; the treatment changes the measurement (by about 2 units on average).\n(d) Expected 10 per face: χ² = (4 + 4 + 1 + 1 + 9 + 9)/10 = **2.8** with 5 degrees of freedom; the critical value at 5 percent is 11.07 and p = **0.73**: **no evidence that the die is unfair**.\n(e) The rejection region is x̄ > 100 + 1.645 × 10/5 = 103.29. If the true mean is 104: β = P(x̄ < 103.29 | μ = 104) = Φ((103.29 - 104)/2) = Φ(-0.355) = **0.361**; **power = 1 - β = 0.639**. (A larger sample or a larger effect raises the power.)\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) x̄ = 3, ȳ = 6.02. S_xy = Σ(x - 3)(y - ȳ) = 19.9 and S_xx = 10, so **β̂1 = 1.99** and **β̂0 = 6.02 - 1.99 × 3 = 0.05**: **ŷ = 0.05 + 1.99x**.\n(b) S_yy = Σ(y - 6.02)² = 39.71; r = 19.9/sqrt(10 × 39.71) = **0.9987** and **R² = 0.9973**: 99.7 percent of the variation in y is explained by the linear relationship.\n(c) ŷ(6) = 0.05 + 11.94 = **11.99**. This is an extrapolation (x = 6 lies outside the observed range 1 to 5): the linear relationship may not continue, so the prediction should be used with caution.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: P² = [[0.8 × 0.8 + 0.2 × 0.4, 0.8 × 0.2 + 0.2 × 0.6],[0.4 × 0.8 + 0.6 × 0.4, 0.4 × 0.2 + 0.6 × 0.6]] = **[[0.72, 0.28],[0.56, 0.44]]**.\nStep 2: The probability of rain two days after a sunny day is **0.28**.\nStep 3: Stationary: π = (π_S, π_R) with π_S = 0.8π_S + 0.4π_R, so 0.2π_S = 0.4π_R and π_S = 2π_R: **π = (2/3, 1/3)**.\nStep 4: The mean return time to the rainy state is 1/π_R = **3 days** (After ten days the chain is already close to π: P^10 first row = (0.6667, 0.3333).)\n(b) Step 1: From π = πP: π_1 = 0.5π_1 + 0.25π_2, π_3 = 0.25π_2 + 0.5π_3 and π_2 = 0.5π_1 + 0.5π_2 + 0.5π_3.\nStep 2: The first gives π_1 = 0.5π_2, the third π_3 = 0.5π_2.\nStep 3: So π ∝ (0.5, 1, 0.5): **π = (0.25, 0.5, 0.25)**.\n(c) For the gambler's ruin with p = 0.4 and q = 0.6, r = q/p = 1.5: P(reach N = 4 from i = 2) = (1 - r^i)/(1 - r^N) = (1 - 2.25)/(1 - 5.0625) = **0.308** (compared with 0.5 for a fair coin): a slightly unfair game makes ruin much more likely.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) The expected number of errors is 1000 × 10^-3 = **1**; P(no error) = (0.999)^1000 = **0.3677**.\n(b) ρ = 8/10 = **0.8**; L = ρ/(1 - ρ) = **4** packets in the system; W = 1/(μ - λ) = **0.5 s** (Little's law: L = λ W = 8 × 0.5 = 4 ✓).\n(c) Posterior = Beta(2 + 7, 2 + 3) = **Beta(9, 5)**, with mean 9/14 = **0.643** (between the prior mean 0.5 and the sample proportion 0.7).\n","exam":"# Probability and Statistics for Engineers: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator, normal and t tables and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks. Useful values: Φ(1) = 0.8413, Φ(1.5) = 0.9332, Φ(2) = 0.9772, z_0.025 = 1.96, z_0.005 = 2.576, t_(3, 0.025) = 3.182, χ²_(1, 0.05) = 3.84.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** If P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5 and A, B are independent, then P(A ∪ B) is\nA) 0.15  B) 0.65  C) 0.8  D) 0.2\n\n**2.** The total probability theorem for a partition B_1, ..., B_n gives P(A) =\nA) Σ P(A | B_i) P(B_i)  B) Σ P(A) P(B_i)  C) Π P(B_i)  D) P(A) + P(B_i)\n\n**3.** The variance of a Bernoulli(p) random variable is\nA) p  B) p²  C) p(1 - p)  D) 1/p\n\n**4.** For X ~ Geometric(p) (number of trials to the first success), E[X] =\nA) p  B) 1/p  C) 1 - p  D) p/(1 - p)\n\n**5.** The exponential distribution is called memoryless because\nA) it has no mean  B) P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t)  C) it is discrete  D) it is symmetric\n\n**6.** If X and Y are independent, Var(X - Y) equals\nA) Var X - Var Y  B) Var X + Var Y  C) 0  D) (Var X)(Var Y)\n\n**7.** Two variables with correlation 0 are\nA) always independent  B) always dependent  C) uncorrelated, but not necessarily independent  D) identical\n\n**8.** By Chebyshev's inequality P(|X - μ| ≥ 2σ) is at most\nA) 1/2  B) 1/4  C) 1/8  D) 0.05\n\n**9.** The sample mean of n independent observations with variance σ² has variance\nA) σ²  B) σ²/n  C) σ/n  D) nσ²\n\n**10.** The normal approximation to Binomial(n, p) works well when\nA) n is small  B) np and n(1 - p) are both large (say above 10)  C) p = 0  D) n = 1\n\n**11.** In a Poisson process with rate λ, the time between events has\nA) a Poisson distribution  B) an exponential distribution with mean 1/λ  C) a uniform distribution  D) a normal distribution\n\n**12.** The maximum likelihood estimator of p for Bernoulli data is\nA) the sample median  B) the sample proportion  C) 1/n  D) the sample variance\n\n**13.** A 99 percent confidence interval is, compared with a 95 percent interval from the same data,\nA) narrower  B) wider  C) the same  D) of random width\n\n**14.** The power of a test is\nA) the probability of a Type I error  B) the probability of rejecting H0 when it is false  C) the p-value  D) the significance level\n\n**15.** A chi-square goodness-of-fit statistic with k categories has how many degrees of freedom (no estimated parameters)?\nA) k  B) k - 1  C) k + 1  D) 1\n\n**16.** For simple linear regression with r = 0.8, R² equals\nA) 0.8  B) 0.64  C) 0.4  D) 0.2\n\n**17.** The stationary distribution π of an irreducible aperiodic Markov chain\nA) depends on the starting state  B) is unique and satisfies π = π P  C) does not exist  D) is always uniform\n\n**18.** For an M/M/1 queue with ρ = 0.5 the mean number in system is\nA) 0.5  B) 1  C) 2  D) 0.25\n\n**19.** The posterior of a Beta(a, b) prior after s successes and f failures is\nA) Beta(a, b)  B) Beta(a + s, b + f)  C) Beta(s, f)  D) Normal\n\n**20.** Statistical significance means\nA) the effect is large  B) the result is unlikely under H0 at the chosen level  C) H0 is true  D) the sample is large\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** A fair die is rolled 4 times. Find the probability of at least one six.\n\n**B2. (5)** Two fair dice are rolled. Given that the first die is even, find the probability that the sum is 8.\n\n**B3. (5)** The time between calls is exponential with rate 0.2 per minute. Find P(T < 5 minutes) and the mean time.\n\n**B4. (5)** Faults occur as a Poisson process with rate 3 per hour. Find the probability of at least 4 faults in an hour.\n\n**B5. (5)** A task time is X ~ N(10, 2²) minutes. Find P(8 < X < 13).\n\n**B6. (5)** A manufacturing process has 30 percent defectives. In a sample of 100 items, use the normal approximation to find the probability that the sample proportion exceeds 0.35.\n\n**B7. (5)** A survey cross-classifies 100 people by preference (A, B) and age group (young, old) with counts young: A 30, B 20; old: A 20, B 30. Test independence at the 5 percent level (χ² with 1 degree of freedom).\n\n**B8. (5)** A fitted regression line is ŷ = 12 + 0.5x with r = 0.8. Interpret the slope and R², and predict y at x = 20. What is the danger of predicting at x = 200?\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Joint distribution.** X ∈ {1, 2} and Y ∈ {1, 2, 3} have the joint pmf p(1,1) = 0.15, p(1,2) = 0.10, p(1,3) = 0.05, p(2,1) = 0.10, p(2,2) = 0.30, p(2,3) = 0.30.\n(a) Find the marginal pmfs [2]. (b) Find E[X], E[Y], E[XY], Cov(X, Y) and the correlation coefficient [4]. (c) Are X and Y independent? [1] (d) Find the conditional pmf of Y given X = 2 and E[Y | X = 2] [3].\n\n**C2. Estimation and inference.** A sensor's defect counts per board in 10 boards were 0, 2, 1, 3, 0, 1, 2, 1, 0, 2 and are modelled as Poisson(λ).\n(a) Derive the MLE of λ and compute it [3]. (b) Estimate the probability that a board has no defects [2]. (c) Give an approximate 95 percent confidence interval for λ using λ̂ ± 1.96 sqrt(λ̂/n) [3]. (d) The specification states λ ≤ 1. Test H0: λ = 1 against H1: λ > 1 at the 5 percent level using the normal approximation (z = (λ̂ - 1)/sqrt(1/n)) [2].\n\n**C3. Markov reliability model.** A machine is Working (W), Degraded (D) or Failed (F, absorbing). Daily transitions: W → W 0.9, W → D 0.08, W → F 0.02; D → W 0.1, D → D 0.7, D → F 0.2; F → F 1.\n(a) Write the transition matrix and find the probability of failure within two days starting from W [3]. (b) Find the expected number of days to failure from W and from D [5]. (c) Is there a non-trivial stationary distribution? [1] (d) Suggest how a maintenance policy changes the model [1].\n\n**C4. Regression analysis.** Calibration data: x = 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and y = 5.1, 9.8, 15.2, 19.9, 25.3.\n(a) Compute the least squares line and r [4]. (b) Compute the residuals and the residual standard error s = sqrt(SSE/(n - 2)) [3]. (c) The slope has standard error 0.035: test H0: β1 = 0 at 5 percent (t_(3, 0.025) = 3.182) and state a 95 percent confidence interval for the slope [3].\n\n**C5. Quality control and the CLT.** A process fills bags with mean 500 g and standard deviation 10 g.\n(a) Find the probability that one bag weighs less than 485 g (assume a normal distribution) [2]. (b) For the mean of 25 bags find the standard error and P(X̄ < 497) [3]. (c) Design a control chart: give the centre line and 3-sigma limits for X̄ with n = 25 [2]. (d) The sample mean shifts to 504 g: find the probability that a sample mean falls outside the limits [3].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **A** · 3 **C** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **C** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 1. 0.3 + 0.5 - 0.15 = 0.65. 6. Variances add for the difference of independent variables. 8. 1/k² with k = 2. 18. L = ρ/(1 - ρ) = 1. 15. The degrees of freedom are k - 1.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** P(no six in 4 rolls) = (5/6)^4 = 0.4823, so P(at least one) = **0.5177**.\n\n**B2.** The first die is even in 18 of the 36 outcomes. Among them the sum is 8 for (2, 6), (4, 4), (6, 2): 3 outcomes. P = 3/18 = **1/6 = 0.167** (the unconditional probability of a sum of 8 is 5/36 = 0.139).\n\n**B3.** P(T < 5) = 1 - e^(-0.2 × 5) = 1 - e^-1 = **0.632**. The mean time is 1/0.2 = **5 minutes**.\n\n**B4.** P(X ≥ 4) = 1 - P(X ≤ 3) = 1 - e^-3 (1 + 3 + 4.5 + 4.5) = 1 - 0.0498 × 13 = 1 - 0.6472 = **0.3528**.\n\n**B5.** z from (8 - 10)/2 = -1 to (13 - 10)/2 = 1.5: Φ(1.5) - Φ(-1) = 0.9332 - 0.1587 = **0.7745**.\n\n**B6.** The standard deviation of the sample proportion is sqrt(0.3 × 0.7/100) = 0.0458, so z = (0.35 - 0.30)/0.0458 = 1.09 and P = **0.138**. (With the continuity correction (0.345) it would be about 0.163; the standard approximation is acceptable.)\n\n**B7.** Expected counts under independence: each cell = 50 × 50/100 = 25. χ² = (30 - 25)²/25 + (20 - 25)²/25 + (20 - 25)²/25 + (30 - 25)²/25 = 4 × 1 = **4.0** with 1 degree of freedom. Since 4.0 > 3.84 (p = 0.0455), **reject independence** at 5 percent: preference and age group are associated (young people favour A, older people favour B).\n\n**B8.** Step 1: The slope: for each unit increase in x, the predicted y increases by **0.5**.\nStep 2: R² = 0.8² = **0.64**: 64 percent of the variation in y is explained by x.\nStep 3: At x = 20: ŷ = 12 + 10 = **22**.\nStep 4: Predicting at x = 200 (ŷ = 112) is an **extrapolation** far outside the range of the data, where the linear relationship may not hold; the prediction is unreliable.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) P(X = 1) = 0.30, **P(X = 2) = 0.70**. P(Y = 1) = **0.25**, P(Y = 2) = **0.40**, P(Y = 3) = **0.35**.\n(b) Step 1: E[X] = 0.3 + 1.4 = **1.7**; E[Y] = 0.25 + 0.8 + 1.05 = **2.1**; E[XY] = 1(0.15 + 0.20 + 0.15) + 2(0.10 + 0.60 + 0.90) = 0.50 + 3.20 = **3.7**.\nStep 2: Cov = 3.7 - 1.7 × 2.1 = 3.7 - 3.57 = **0.13**.\nStep 3: Var(X) = 0.21 and Var(Y) = E[Y²] - 4.41 = (0.25 + 1.6 + 3.15) - 4.41 = 0.59, so **ρ = 0.13/sqrt(0.21 × 0.59) = 0.369**.\n(c) **Not independent**: p(1,1) = 0.15 but P(X = 1) P(Y = 1) = 0.3 × 0.25 = 0.075.\n(d) Given X = 2: P(Y = y | X = 2) = p(2, y)/0.7 = **1/7, 3/7, 3/7** for y = 1, 2, 3. E[Y | X = 2] = 1/7 + 6/7 + 9/7 = 16/7 = **2.286**.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) L(λ) = Π e^(-λ) λ^(x_i)/x_i!, so ln L = -nλ + (Σx) ln λ - Σ ln x_i!. Setting the derivative to zero: -n + Σx/λ = 0, so **λ̂ = x̄**. The sum is 12 and n = 10: **λ̂ = 1.2** defects per board.\n(b) P(X = 0) = e^(-λ̂) = e^(-1.2) = **0.301**.\n(c) SE = sqrt(1.2/10) = 0.346: 1.2 ± 1.96 × 0.346 = **(0.521, 1.879)**.\n(d) z = (1.2 - 1)/sqrt(1/10) = 0.2/0.316 = **0.63**; the one-sided critical value is 1.645 (p = 0.26): **do not reject H0**: there is no significant evidence that the defect rate exceeds 1 (the interval from (c) also contains 1).\n\n**C3.**\n(a) P = [[0.90, 0.08, 0.02], [0.10, 0.70, 0.20], [0, 0, 1]]. Probability of being failed within two days from W: P(F after 2 steps) = 0.02 + 0.9 × 0.02 + 0.08 × 0.2 = 0.02 + 0.018 + 0.016 = **0.054** (the second row of P² has F-entry 0.054).\n(b) Let t_W, t_D be the expected times to absorption. t_W = 1 + 0.9 t_W + 0.08 t_D and t_D = 1 + 0.1 t_W + 0.7 t_D. The second gives 0.3 t_D = 1 + 0.1 t_W, so t_D = (1 + 0.1 t_W)/0.3. Substituting into the first: 0.1 t_W = 1 + 0.08(1 + 0.1 t_W)/0.3 = 1.2667 + 0.02667 t_W, so 0.07333 t_W = 1.2667 and **t_W = 17.27 days**, **t_D = 9.09 days**.\n(c) The only stationary distribution is the degenerate one concentrated on the absorbing state F: π = (0, 0, 1). The chain is not irreducible, so there is no distribution with all states positive.\n(d) **Preventive maintenance** adds an action that moves D (or W) to a better state with some probability, which lowers the transition probabilities to F and increases the expected time to failure; the model can then be used to compare policies by cost (a Markov decision process).\n\n**C4.**\n(a) x̄ = 6, ȳ = 15.06; S_xy = 101, S_xx = 40: **β̂1 = 2.525**, **β̂0 = 15.06 - 2.525 × 6 = -0.09**: **ŷ = -0.09 + 2.525x**. The correlation r = **0.9997** (R² = 0.9994).\n(b) Fitted values: 4.96, 10.01, 15.06, 20.11, 25.16. Residuals y - ŷ: **0.14, -0.21, 0.14, -0.21, 0.14** (sum 0). SSE = 0.0196 + 0.0441 + 0.0196 + 0.0441 + 0.0196 = 0.147, so **s = sqrt(0.147/3) = 0.221**.\n(c) t = 2.525/0.035 = **72.1** ≫ 3.182: **reject H0**, the slope is significantly different from 0 (p < 0.001). 95 percent CI for the slope: 2.525 ± 3.182 × 0.035 = **(2.41, 2.64)**.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) z = (485 - 500)/10 = -1.5: P = **0.0668**.\n(b) SE = 10/sqrt 25 = **2**; P(X̄ < 497) = P(Z < -1.5) = **0.0668**.\n(c) Centre line **500 g**; control limits 500 ± 3 × 2 = **494 g and 506 g** (the 3-sigma limits give a false alarm probability of 0.0027 per sample).\n(d) With the mean shifted to 504 g: the sample mean ~ N(504, 2²). P(X̄ > 506) = P(Z > 1) = 0.1587 and P(X̄ < 494) = P(Z < -5) ≈ 0, so the probability of an out-of-limits point per sample is about **0.159**: the average run length to detection is 1/0.159 ≈ 6.3 samples.\n","notes":"# Probability and Statistics for Engineers: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nEngineers deal with uncertainty everywhere: noise in signals, component failures, network traffic, measurement error, machine-learning data. **Probability** gives the mathematical model of randomness; **statistics** uses data to learn about the model. Notation: P(A) probability of an event, E[X] expected value, Var(X) variance, σ standard deviation, X ~ N(μ, σ²) means \"X is normally distributed\", x̄ the sample mean, s the sample standard deviation.\n\n## Chapter 1. Probability Foundations\n\n### 1.1 Sample spaces, events and axioms\nA **sample space** S is the set of all possible outcomes; an **event** is a subset of S. Kolmogorov's axioms: (1) P(A) ≥ 0; (2) P(S) = 1; (3) for mutually exclusive events P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B). Consequences: P(A') = 1 - P(A); P(∅) = 0; **P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A ∩ B)**; if A ⊆ B then P(A) ≤ P(B). For equally likely outcomes P(A) = |A|/|S|, and counting (permutations, combinations) does the work.\n- **Examples.** Two aces drawn without replacement from a 52-card deck: (4/52)(3/51) = 1/221 = 0.00452. A five-card full house: 3744/2,598,960 = 0.00144. A 6/49 lottery jackpot: 1/13,983,816. **Birthday problem:** with 23 people the probability that some two share a birthday is 1 - Π(365 - i)/365 = 0.507.\n\n### 1.2 Conditional probability, independence, total probability, Bayes\n- **Conditional probability:** P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B). **Multiplication rule:** P(A ∩ B) = P(B) P(A | B).\n- **Independence:** P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B) (equivalently P(A | B) = P(A)). Independent does not mean mutually exclusive.\n- **Law of total probability:** for a partition B_1, ..., B_n: P(A) = Σ P(A | B_i) P(B_i).\n- **Bayes' theorem:** P(B_i | A) = P(A | B_i) P(B_i)/P(A).\n- **Example (medical test).** Prevalence 1 percent, sensitivity P(+ | D) = 0.95, false-positive rate P(+ | not D) = 0.10: P(D | +) = 0.95 × 0.01/(0.95 × 0.01 + 0.10 × 0.99) = 0.0876. Even a good test gives mostly false positives for a rare condition (the base-rate effect).\n- **Example (factories).** A makes 50 percent of the items with 2 percent defects, B 30 percent with 3 percent, C 20 percent with 5 percent: P(defect) = 0.01 + 0.009 + 0.01 = 0.029, and P(A | defect) = 0.01/0.029 = 0.345.\n- **Reliability of systems:** components in series (all must work) multiply: 0.9 × 0.8 × 0.95 = 0.684; in parallel (at least one) use the complement: 1 - (0.1)(0.2)(0.05) = 0.999 (assuming independence).\n\n## Chapter 2. Random Variables\n\n### 2.1 Discrete random variables\nA **random variable** X is a function from outcomes to numbers. A discrete X has a **probability mass function** p(x) = P(X = x) with Σ p(x) = 1, and a **cumulative distribution function** F(x) = P(X ≤ x).\n- **Expected value** E[X] = Σ x p(x). **Linearity:** E[aX + bY + c] = a E[X] + b E[Y] + c (always, even for dependent variables). E[g(X)] = Σ g(x) p(x).\n- **Variance** Var(X) = E[(X - μ)²] = E[X²] - (E[X])². Var(aX + b) = a² Var(X). For independent X and Y, Var(X + Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y). The standard deviation σ = sqrt(Var).\n- **Example:** one fair die: E = 3.5, Var = 35/12 = 2.92. The sum of two dice: E = 7, Var = 35/6 = 5.83.\n- **Indicator variables** make hard expectations easy: the expected number of fixed points of a random permutation of n items is n × (1/n) = 1; the coupon-collector expectation for n = 6 faces of a die is 6(1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/6) = 14.7 throws to see every face.\n\n### 2.2 Important discrete distributions\n- **Bernoulli(p):** one trial, E = p, Var = p(1 - p).\n- **Binomial(n, p):** number of successes in n independent trials: P(X = k) = C(n, k) p^k (1 - p)^(n-k); E = np, Var = np(1 - p). Binomial(10, 0.3): P(X = 3) = 0.2668, P(X ≤ 2) = 0.3828, E = 3, Var = 2.1.\n- **Geometric(p):** number of trials to the first success: P(X = k) = (1 - p)^(k-1) p; E = 1/p, Var = (1 - p)/p². For p = 0.2: P(X = 5) = 0.0819, E = 5. Memoryless.\n- **Poisson(λ):** counts of rare events in a fixed interval: P(X = k) = e^(-λ) λ^k/k!; E = Var = λ. For λ = 4: P(X = 2) = 0.1465, P(X ≥ 1) = 0.9817. It approximates Binomial(n, p) when n is large and p small (λ = np).\n- **Hypergeometric** (sampling without replacement), **negative binomial**, **uniform** on {1, ..., n}.\n\n### 2.3 Continuous random variables\nA continuous X has a **probability density function** f(x) ≥ 0 with ∫ f = 1; P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = ∫(a to b) f dx; P(X = x) = 0 for every single x. F(x) = ∫(-∞ to x) f; f = F'. E[X] = ∫ x f(x) dx; Var(X) = E[X²] - μ².\n- **Example:** f(x) = x²/9 on [0, 3]: P(1 ≤ X ≤ 2) = (8 - 1)/27 = 0.259; E = 2.25; E[X²] = 5.4, Var = 0.3375.\n- **Uniform(a, b):** f = 1/(b - a); E = (a + b)/2; Var = (b - a)²/12.\n- **Exponential(λ):** f = λ e^(-λx), x ≥ 0; F = 1 - e^(-λx); E = 1/λ; Var = 1/λ². **Memoryless:** P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t). Models lifetimes and inter-arrival times: for a mean of 2 hours, P(T > 3) = e^(-1.5) = 0.223 and the median is 2 ln 2 = 1.386 hours.\n- **Normal N(μ, σ²):** f = (1/(σ sqrt(2π))) exp(-(x - μ)²/(2σ²)). **Standardise:** Z = (X - μ)/σ ~ N(0, 1); use tables or software for Φ(z). **68-95-99.7 rule:** within 1, 2, 3 standard deviations. For X ~ N(100, 15²): P(X > 130) = P(Z > 2) = 0.0228; P(85 < X < 115) = 0.6827; the 95th percentile is 100 + 1.645 × 15 = 124.7. Sums of independent normals are normal: N(μ1 + μ2, σ1² + σ2²). Noise in electronic systems is usually modelled as Gaussian.\n- **Others:** gamma, chi-square, Student's t, F, beta, Weibull (reliability), log-normal.\n- **Transformations:** if Y = g(X) with g monotonic, f_Y(y) = f_X(g^-1(y)) |d g^-1/dy|. For X ~ U(0, 1) and Y = X², f_Y(y) = 1/(2 sqrt y) on (0, 1) and E[Y] = 1/3.\n\n## Chapter 3. Several Random Variables\n\n### 3.1 Joint, marginal and conditional distributions\nFor discrete X, Y: p(x, y) = P(X = x, Y = y); marginals p_X(x) = Σ_y p(x, y); conditional p(y | x) = p(x, y)/p_X(x). X and Y are **independent** iff p(x, y) = p_X(x) p_Y(y) for all x, y. Continuous: joint density f(x, y), marginals by integration.\n- **Covariance:** Cov(X, Y) = E[XY] - E[X] E[Y]. **Correlation** ρ = Cov/(σ_X σ_Y) ∈ [-1, 1]. Independent implies uncorrelated, **not the converse**. Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2 Cov(X, Y).\n- **Example.** p(0,0) = 0.1, p(0,1) = 0.2, p(0,2) = 0.1, p(1,0) = 0.2, p(1,1) = 0.3, p(1,2) = 0.1. Marginals: P(X = 0) = 0.4, P(X = 1) = 0.6; P(Y = 0, 1, 2) = 0.3, 0.5, 0.2. E[X] = 0.6, E[Y] = 0.9, E[XY] = 0.5, so Cov = 0.5 - 0.54 = -0.04, ρ = -0.117. They are not independent (p(0,0) = 0.1 ≠ 0.4 × 0.3 = 0.12).\n\n### 3.2 Inequalities and limit theorems\n- **Markov:** for X ≥ 0, P(X ≥ a) ≤ E[X]/a. **Chebyshev:** P(|X - μ| ≥ kσ) ≤ 1/k². With μ = 50 and σ = 5: P(|X - 50| ≥ 15) ≤ 1/9.\n- **Law of large numbers:** the sample mean converges to μ as n → ∞ (justifies Monte Carlo estimation and relative frequencies).\n- **Central limit theorem (CLT):** for i.i.d. variables with mean μ and variance σ², the standardised mean (X̄ - μ)/(σ/sqrt n) tends to N(0, 1). So X̄ is approximately N(μ, σ²/n) for large n (n ≥ 30 as a rule of thumb). Examples: n = 36, μ = 50, σ = 12: P(X̄ > 53) = P(Z > 1.5) = 0.0668. **Normal approximation to the binomial** with a continuity correction: for X ~ Bin(100, 0.5), P(X ≥ 60) ≈ P(Z > (59.5 - 50)/5) = P(Z > 1.9) = 0.0287 (exact 0.0284).\n- The CLT explains why noise and measurement errors, which are sums of many small effects, are Gaussian.\n\n## Chapter 4. Random Processes: Poisson and Markov Chains\n\n### 4.1 Poisson process\nEvents occur at rate λ per unit time: counts in disjoint intervals are independent, N(t) ~ Poisson(λt), and inter-arrival times are independent Exponential(λ). If calls arrive at 3 per minute, P(no call in a minute) = e^(-3) = 0.0498 and P(next call takes more than 30 s) = e^(-1.5) = 0.223. Used for traffic, failures, photon counts and queues.\n\n### 4.2 Discrete-time Markov chains\nA **Markov chain** has the property P(X_(n+1) | X_n, X_(n-1), ...) = P(X_(n+1) | X_n). It is described by a **transition matrix** P (rows sum to 1): the distribution after n steps is π_0 P^n. A **stationary distribution** π satisfies π P = π (with Σ π = 1). For P = [[0.8, 0.2],[0.4, 0.6]] (sunny/rainy): P² = [[0.72, 0.28],[0.56, 0.44]] and π = (2/3, 1/3); the mean return time to state i is 1/π_i. An irreducible aperiodic chain converges to its unique stationary distribution. **Absorbing chains** model gambling and reliability: the fair gambler's ruin starting with 2 units, target 4, has win probability 2/4 = 0.5. Applications: PageRank, queues, protocols, hidden Markov models in speech recognition.\n\n### 4.3 Queues (M/M/1)\nPoisson arrivals at rate λ and exponential service at rate μ: utilisation ρ = λ/μ < 1; mean number in system L = ρ/(1 - ρ); mean time W = 1/(μ - λ) (Little's law L = λW). With λ = 8 and μ = 10 per second: ρ = 0.8, L = 4, W = 0.5 s; delays blow up as ρ → 1.\n\n## Chapter 5. Descriptive Statistics\n\n- **Centre:** mean x̄ = Σx/n, median (robust to outliers), mode. **Spread:** range, **sample variance** s² = Σ(x - x̄)²/(n - 1), standard deviation, **quartiles** Q1, Q3, interquartile range IQR = Q3 - Q1. **Outliers** (rule of thumb): below Q1 - 1.5 IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 IQR.\n- **Example.** Data 12, 15, 11, 18, 14, 15, 13, 40, 16, 14: mean 16.8, median 14.5, bimodal (14 and 15), s² = 70.4, s = 8.39, Q1 = 13.25, Q3 = 15.75, IQR = 2.5, fences 9.5 and 19.5, so 40 is an outlier. The outlier pulls the mean up but not the median.\n- **Graphs:** histogram, box plot, scatter plot, normal probability plot. Choose the summary to match the distribution (skewed data: median and IQR).\n\n## Chapter 6. Estimation\n\n### 6.1 Point estimators\nA statistic θ̂ estimates a parameter θ. **Unbiased:** E[θ̂] = θ; **consistent:** θ̂ → θ as n grows; **efficient:** small variance (Cramér-Rao bound). The sample mean is unbiased for μ; **s² with the n - 1 divisor is unbiased for σ²** (dividing by n underestimates). Standard error of the mean: σ/sqrt n.\n- **Method of moments:** equate sample and population moments (for Exponential(λ): λ̂ = 1/x̄).\n- **Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE):** choose θ to maximise the likelihood L(θ) = Π f(x_i; θ) (or the log-likelihood). Bernoulli: p̂ = x̄ (the sample proportion); Exponential: λ̂ = 1/x̄; Normal: μ̂ = x̄ and σ̂² = Σ(x - x̄)²/n (biased); Poisson: λ̂ = x̄. MLEs are consistent and asymptotically efficient.\n\n### 6.2 Confidence intervals\nA 95 percent confidence interval is produced by a procedure that captures the true parameter in 95 percent of repeated samples (it does not say the parameter is in this particular interval with probability 0.95).\n- **Mean, σ known (or large n):** x̄ ± z_(α/2) σ/sqrt n (z = 1.96 for 95 percent, 2.576 for 99 percent).\n- **Mean, σ unknown, normal data:** x̄ ± t_(n-1, α/2) s/sqrt n. n = 25, x̄ = 52, s = 10: t_(24, 0.025) = 2.064, interval 52 ± 4.13 = (47.87, 56.13).\n- **Proportion:** p̂ ± z sqrt(p̂(1 - p̂)/n). 230 of 400: p̂ = 0.575, SE = 0.0247, interval (0.527, 0.623).\n- **Sample size** for margin of error E: n = z² p(1 - p)/E² (using p = 0.5 if unknown): for E = 0.03 at 95 percent, n = 1068.\n- Width shrinks as 1/sqrt n: four times the data halves the width.\n\n## Chapter 7. Hypothesis Testing\n\n### 7.1 The framework\nState H0 (null) and H1 (alternative); choose the significance level α (usually 0.05); compute a **test statistic**; get the **p-value** (the probability under H0 of a result at least as extreme as observed); reject H0 if p < α. **Type I error:** rejecting a true H0 (probability α). **Type II error:** failing to reject a false H0 (probability β); **power** = 1 - β. A small p-value is evidence against H0; it is not the probability that H0 is true. Statistical significance is not practical significance.\n\n### 7.2 Common tests\n- **z-test for a mean** (σ known): z = (x̄ - μ0)/(σ/sqrt n). H0: μ = 100, n = 49, x̄ = 103, σ = 14: z = 1.5, two-sided p = 0.134, so do not reject at 5 percent.\n- **t-test** (σ unknown): t = (x̄ - μ0)/(s/sqrt n) with n - 1 degrees of freedom; **paired t-test** for before/after data; **two-sample t-test** (Welch) for two means.\n- **Test for a proportion**, **chi-square goodness-of-fit:** χ² = Σ(O - E)²/E. A die rolled 60 times with counts 8, 12, 9, 11, 13, 7 against expected 10 each: χ² = 2.8 with 5 degrees of freedom (critical value 11.07, p = 0.73), so no evidence that the die is unfair. **Chi-square test of independence** for contingency tables. **ANOVA** compares several means.\n- **A/B testing** applies two-sample proportion or mean tests to compare designs; plan the sample size to achieve the required power, and beware repeated looks at the data (multiple testing).\n\n## Chapter 8. Regression and Correlation\n\n**Simple linear regression:** model y = β0 + β1 x + ε with errors of mean 0 and constant variance. **Least squares:** β̂1 = S_xy/S_xx = Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ)/Σ(x - x̄)², β̂0 = ȳ - β̂1 x̄. The **correlation coefficient** r = S_xy/sqrt(S_xx S_yy) and **R² = r²** is the fraction of variance explained. Residuals e = y - ŷ should have no pattern (check with a residual plot). Example: x = 1 to 5, y = 2.1, 3.9, 6.2, 7.8, 10.1 gives ŷ = 0.05 + 1.99x, r = 0.9987, R² = 0.9973. Correlation is not causation; extrapolation is risky. Extensions: multiple regression, polynomial regression, logistic regression (classification), confidence intervals for the slope. This is the basis of curve fitting, calibration of sensors and machine learning.\n\n## Chapter 9. Bayesian Inference (introduction)\nTreat the parameter as random: posterior ∝ likelihood × prior. For a Bernoulli probability p with a Beta(a, b) prior and s successes in n trials the posterior is Beta(a + s, b + n - s). With a Beta(2, 2) prior and 7 successes in 10 trials: Beta(9, 5), mean 9/14 = 0.643 (the MLE is 0.7; the prior shrinks the estimate), MAP = 8/12 = 0.667. Credible intervals; priors encode earlier knowledge; Bayesian filters (Kalman filter) are central to engineering.\n\n## Chapter 10. Engineering Applications\n- **Communications:** bit error rate p = 10^-3 and a 1000-bit packet: P(no errors) = (1 - p)^1000 = 0.3677 ≈ e^-1 (Poisson approximation), expected errors 1.\n- **Reliability:** exponential lifetime with mean time to failure MTTF; series/parallel structures; Weibull.\n- **Quality control:** control charts (x̄ ± 3σ/sqrt n), acceptance sampling, six-sigma.\n- **Signal processing and machine learning:** Gaussian noise, estimation theory, Bayes classifiers, cross-validation.\n\n## Chapter 11. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Treating independent and mutually exclusive events as the same thing.\n2. Dividing by n instead of n - 1 for the sample variance; using the population formula for a sample.\n3. Forgetting to standardise (or to use the continuity correction) in normal approximations.\n4. Saying \"the probability that the true mean lies in this interval is 95 percent\".\n5. Interpreting a p-value as the probability that the null hypothesis is true, or accepting H0 instead of \"failing to reject\".\n6. Confusing correlation with causation, and using regression far outside the data range.\n7. Ignoring the base rate in Bayes problems.\n8. Computing Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y without independence (the covariance term).\n","plan":"# Probability and Statistics for Engineers: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 5 to 6 problems, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Learn this course with a computer in hand: simulate every probability you compute (Python with NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib; or R) and compare the simulated frequency with your answer. That habit builds intuition faster than any amount of algebra.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Sheldon Ross, *A First Course in Probability* and *Introduction to Probability and Statistics for Engineers and Scientists*.\n- Dimitri Bertsekas and John Tsitsiklis, *Introduction to Probability* (the MIT text, rigorous and clear).\n- Douglas Montgomery and George Runger, *Applied Statistics and Probability for Engineers* (many engineering examples).\n- Larry Wasserman, *All of Statistics* (compact, for later reading); Joseph Blitzstein and Jessica Hwang, *Introduction to Probability* (free online, with Harvard Stat 110 lectures).\n- Allen Downey, *Think Stats* and *Think Bayes* (free, code-based).\n\n## Free courses\n- Harvard Stat 110 (Blitzstein): excellent lectures and problem sets.\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.041 Probabilistic Systems Analysis and 18.05 Introduction to Probability and Statistics.\n- Khan Academy Statistics and Probability; StatQuest videos (Josh Starmer) for intuition about tests and regression.\n- Seeing Theory (Brown University) for interactive visualisations.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Sample spaces, axioms, counting, conditional probability (Ch 1.1, 1.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1, Q2. Simulate the birthday problem.\n\n**Week 2: Independence, total probability, Bayes, reliability (Ch 1.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2, Q3. Checkpoint: set up any Bayes problem with a tree diagram.\n\n**Week 3: Discrete random variables, expectation, variance (Ch 2.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q6.\n\n**Week 4: Binomial, geometric, Poisson (Ch 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q5, Q7. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Continuous distributions, uniform, exponential (Ch 2.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1, Q2, Q6.\n\n**Week 6: The normal distribution and standardisation (Ch 2.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q3. Plot histograms of simulated sums of uniform variables.\n\n**Week 7: Joint distributions, covariance, correlation (Ch 3.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4.\n\n**Week 8: Inequalities, LLN, CLT (Ch 3.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q5. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 9: Poisson process, Markov chains, queues (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6, Q7. Simulate a Markov chain and watch it converge to its stationary distribution.\n\n**Week 10: Descriptive statistics and estimation (Ch 5, 6.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2.\n\n**Week 11: Confidence intervals (Ch 6.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3. Simulate 1000 intervals and count how many contain the true mean.\n\n**Week 12: Hypothesis testing (Ch 7).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4. Practise choosing the right test.\n\n**Week 13: Regression and Bayesian inference (Ch 8, 9).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5, Q7(c). Fit a line to real sensor data and examine the residuals.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet and statistical tables).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can compute probabilities with counting, conditioning, independence and Bayes' theorem.\n- [ ] I can find the pmf/pdf, mean and variance of the standard distributions and know when each applies.\n- [ ] I can standardise and use normal tables for probabilities and percentiles.\n- [ ] I can work with joint distributions, covariance and correlation.\n- [ ] I can state and apply the LLN and CLT, including the normal approximation to the binomial.\n- [ ] I can model arrivals with a Poisson process and analyse a simple Markov chain.\n- [ ] I can compute and interpret point estimates and confidence intervals.\n- [ ] I can run z, t and chi-square tests, and interpret p-values, errors and power.\n- [ ] I can fit and interpret a simple linear regression.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet and tables.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Draw a picture (tree, Venn diagram, sketch of the density with the shaded area) before computing.\n2. Identify the distribution and its parameters in words first (\"number of successes in 10 independent trials with probability 0.3: binomial\").\n3. Always sanity-check: probabilities lie in [0, 1], variances are positive, an answer must be consistent with the mean.\n4. Verify analytically derived results with a quick simulation.\n5. When interpreting inference, say exactly what the p-value or interval means, and what it does not.\n","test":"# Probability and Statistics for Engineers: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator and normal tables allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Useful values: Φ(1) = 0.8413, Φ(2) = 0.9772, Φ(0.5) = 0.6915, z_0.025 = 1.96, z_0.05 = 1.645, z_0.10 = 1.2816.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** For any two events, P(A ∪ B) equals\nA) P(A) + P(B)  B) P(A) + P(B) - P(A ∩ B)  C) P(A) P(B)  D) P(A) - P(B)\n\n**2.** Events A and B are independent if\nA) they cannot both occur  B) P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B)  C) P(A) = P(B)  D) P(A ∪ B) = 1\n\n**3.** The probability of two heads in two independent fair coin flips is\nA) 1/2  B) 1/4  C) 1/3  D) 3/4\n\n**4.** Bayes' theorem expresses P(A | B) as\nA) P(B | A) P(A)/P(B)  B) P(A) P(B)  C) P(A ∩ B) P(B)  D) P(B)/P(A)\n\n**5.** The expected value of one roll of a fair six-sided die is\nA) 3  B) 3.5  C) 4  D) 6\n\n**6.** If Var(X) = 3, then Var(2X) equals\nA) 6  B) 9  C) 12  D) 3\n\n**7.** The mean of a Binomial(n, p) variable is\nA) p  B) np  C) np(1 - p)  D) n/p\n\n**8.** For a Poisson random variable\nA) the mean is twice the variance  B) the mean equals the variance  C) the variance is 1  D) the mean is zero\n\n**9.** The mean of an exponential variable with rate λ is\nA) λ  B) 1/λ  C) λ²  D) 1/λ²\n\n**10.** About what fraction of a normal distribution lies within one standard deviation of the mean?\nA) 50 percent  B) 68 percent  C) 95 percent  D) 99.7 percent\n\n**11.** For a standard normal variable Z, P(Z < 0) equals\nA) 0  B) 0.25  C) 0.5  D) 1\n\n**12.** The standard error of the sample mean of n observations with standard deviation σ is\nA) σ  B) σ/n  C) σ/sqrt n  D) σ²/n\n\n**13.** The central limit theorem says that the distribution of the sample mean tends to\nA) uniform  B) normal  C) exponential  D) binomial\n\n**14.** The unbiased sample variance divides the sum of squared deviations by\nA) n  B) n - 1  C) n + 1  D) n²\n\n**15.** The critical z value for a 95 percent confidence interval is about\nA) 1.28  B) 1.645  C) 1.96  D) 2.58\n\n**16.** A p-value is\nA) the probability that H0 is true  B) the probability of data at least as extreme as observed, assuming H0  C) the significance level  D) the power\n\n**17.** A Type I error is\nA) failing to reject a false H0  B) rejecting a true H0  C) an arithmetic mistake  D) rejecting a false H0\n\n**18.** The correlation coefficient always lies in\nA) [0, 1]  B) [-1, 1]  C) (-∞, ∞)  D) [0, 100]\n\n**19.** In least squares regression y = β0 + β1 x, a negative slope means\nA) y tends to decrease as x increases  B) y is negative  C) the fit is poor  D) x is negative\n\n**20.** A stationary distribution π of a Markov chain satisfies\nA) π P = π  B) P π = 0  C) π + P = 1  D) P² = P\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** Machine M1 makes 60 percent of the items, of which 3 percent are defective; machine M2 makes 40 percent, of which 5 percent are defective. (a) Find P(defective) [3]. (b) An item is found defective; find P(it came from M1) [3]. (c) Two items are selected at random from a large production run (independent): find the probability that both are defective [2]. (d) Find the probability that exactly one of the two is defective [2].\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** (a) X takes the values 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.2, 0.5, 0.3. Find E[X], E[X²] and Var(X) [4]. (b) X ~ Binomial(5, 0.4): find P(X = 2) [2]. (c) X ~ Poisson(2): find P(X = 0) and P(X ≥ 2) [4].\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** X ~ N(50, 8²). (a) Find P(X < 42) [2]. (b) Find P(X > 66) [2]. (c) Find P(46 < X < 58) [3]. (d) Find the 90th percentile [1]. (e) For a sample of n = 64 independent observations find P(X̄ > 51) [2].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** A sample of n = 36 measurements has mean 48 and standard deviation 9. (a) Find the standard error [1]. (b) Find the 95 percent confidence interval for the mean (t_35, 0.025 = 2.03) [3]. (c) Test H0: μ = 50 against H1: μ ≠ 50 at the 5 percent level: give the test statistic, the decision and the approximate p-value (0.19) [4]. (d) Explain the meaning of a 95 percent confidence interval [2].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **A** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **C** · 12 **C** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **C** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **A** · 20 **A**\n\nBrief reasons: 3. (1/2)(1/2). 6. Var(aX) = a² Var(X) = 4 × 3. 8. Both equal λ. 9. E[X] = 1/λ. 12. SE = σ/√n. 14. The n - 1 divisor makes it unbiased.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) P(D) = 0.6 × 0.03 + 0.4 × 0.05 = 0.018 + 0.020 = **0.038**.\n(b) P(M1 | D) = 0.018/0.038 = **0.474**.\n(c) P(both defective) = 0.038² = **0.001444**.\n(d) P(exactly one) = 2 × 0.038 × 0.962 = **0.0731**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) E[X] = 0.2 + 1.0 + 0.9 = **2.1**; E[X²] = 0.2 + 2.0 + 2.7 = **4.9**; Var = 4.9 - 4.41 = **0.49**.\n(b) P(X = 2) = C(5, 2)(0.4)²(0.6)³ = 10 × 0.16 × 0.216 = **0.3456**.\n(c) P(X = 0) = e^-2 = **0.1353**; P(X ≥ 2) = 1 - e^-2(1 + 2) = 1 - 0.406 = **0.594**.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) z = (42 - 50)/8 = -1: P = **0.1587**.\n(b) z = (66 - 50)/8 = 2: P = 1 - 0.9772 = **0.0228**.\n(c) z from -0.5 to 1: 0.8413 - 0.3085 = **0.5328**.\n(d) 50 + 1.2816 × 8 = **60.25**.\n(e) The standard error is 8/sqrt 64 = 1, so P(X̄ > 51) = P(Z > 1) = **0.1587**.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) SE = 9/sqrt 36 = **1.5**.\n(b) 48 ± 2.03 × 1.5 = 48 ± 3.05: **(44.95, 51.05)**.\n(c) t = (48 - 50)/1.5 = **-1.33** with 35 degrees of freedom; the critical value is ±2.03 and the two-sided p-value is about **0.19** > 0.05: **do not reject H0** (no significant evidence that the mean differs from 50; indeed 50 lies inside the interval of part (b)).\n(d) If we repeated the sampling many times and built an interval each time the same way, about 95 percent of these intervals would contain the true mean. It does not mean there is a 95 percent probability that the true mean lies in this one computed interval.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"For any two events, P(A ∪ B) equals","options":["P(A) + P(B)","P(A) + P(B) - P(A ∩ B)","P(A) P(B)","P(A) - P(B)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Events A and B are independent if","options":["they cannot both occur","P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B)","P(A) = P(B)","P(A ∪ B) = 1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The probability of two heads in two independent fair coin flips is","options":["1/2","1/4","1/3","3/4"],"answer":1,"explain":"(1/2)(1/2).","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Bayes' theorem expresses P(A | B) as","options":["P(B | A) P(A)/P(B)","P(A) P(B)","P(A ∩ B) P(B)","P(B)/P(A)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The expected value of one roll of a fair six-sided die is","options":["3","3.5","4","6"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If Var(X) = 3, then Var(2X) equals","options":["6","9","12","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Var(aX) = a² Var(X) = 4 × 3.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The mean of a Binomial(n, p) variable is","options":["p","np","np(1 - p)","n/p"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"For a Poisson random variable","options":["the mean is twice the variance","the mean equals the variance","the variance is 1","the mean is zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both equal λ.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The mean of an exponential variable with rate λ is","options":["λ","1/λ","λ²","1/λ²"],"answer":1,"explain":"E[X] = 1/λ.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"About what fraction of a normal distribution lies within one standard deviation of the mean?","options":["50 percent","68 percent","95 percent","99.7 percent"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"For a standard normal variable Z, P(Z < 0) equals","options":["0","0.25","0.5","1"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The standard error of the sample mean of n observations with standard deviation σ is","options":["σ","σ/n","σ/sqrt n","σ²/n"],"answer":2,"explain":"SE = σ/√n.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The central limit theorem says that the distribution of the sample mean tends to","options":["uniform","normal","exponential","binomial"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The unbiased sample variance divides the sum of squared deviations by","options":["n","n - 1","n + 1","n²"],"answer":1,"explain":"The n - 1 divisor makes it unbiased.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The critical z value for a 95 percent confidence interval is about","options":["1.28","1.645","1.96","2.58"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A p-value is","options":["the probability that H0 is true","the probability of data at least as extreme as observed, assuming H0","the significance level","the power"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A Type I error is","options":["failing to reject a false H0","rejecting a true H0","an arithmetic mistake","rejecting a false H0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The correlation coefficient always lies in","options":["[0, 1]","[-1, 1]","(-∞, ∞)","[0, 100]"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In least squares regression y = β0 + β1 x, a negative slope means","options":["y tends to decrease as x increases","y is negative","the fit is poor","x is negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A stationary distribution π of a Markov chain satisfies","options":["π P = π","P π = 0","π + P = 1","P² = P"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5 and A, B are independent, then P(A ∪ B) is","options":["0.15","0.65","0.8","0.2"],"answer":1,"explain":"0.3 + 0.5 - 0.15 = 0.65.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The total probability theorem for a partition B_1, ..., B_n gives P(A) =","options":["Σ P(A | B_i) P(B_i)","Σ P(A) P(B_i)","Π P(B_i)","P(A) + P(B_i)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The variance of a Bernoulli(p) random variable is","options":["p","p²","p(1 - p)","1/p"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For X ~ Geometric(p) (number of trials to the first success), E[X] =","options":["p","1/p","1 - p","p/(1 - p)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The exponential distribution is called memoryless because","options":["it has no mean","P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t)","it is discrete","it is symmetric"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"If X and Y are independent, Var(X - Y) equals","options":["Var X - Var Y","Var X + Var Y","0","(Var X)(Var Y)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Variances add for the difference of independent variables.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Two variables with correlation 0 are","options":["always independent","always dependent","uncorrelated, but not necessarily independent","identical"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"By Chebyshev's inequality P(|X - μ| ≥ 2σ) is at most","options":["1/2","1/4","1/8","0.05"],"answer":1,"explain":"1/k² with k = 2.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The sample mean of n independent observations with variance σ² has variance","options":["σ²","σ²/n","σ/n","nσ²"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The normal approximation to Binomial(n, p) works well when","options":["n is small","np and n(1 - p) are both large (say above 10)","p = 0","n = 1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a Poisson process with rate λ, the time between events has","options":["a Poisson distribution","an exponential distribution with mean 1/λ","a uniform distribution","a normal distribution"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The maximum likelihood estimator of p for Bernoulli data is","options":["the sample median","the sample proportion","1/n","the sample variance"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 99 percent confidence interval is, compared with a 95 percent interval from the same data,","options":["narrower","wider","the same","of random width"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The power of a test is","options":["the probability of a Type I error","the probability of rejecting H0 when it is false","the p-value","the significance level"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A chi-square goodness-of-fit statistic with k categories has how many degrees of freedom (no estimated parameters)?","options":["k","k - 1","k + 1","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For simple linear regression with r = 0.8, R² equals","options":["0.8","0.64","0.4","0.2"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The stationary distribution π of an irreducible aperiodic Markov chain","options":["depends on the starting state","is unique and satisfies π = π P","does not exist","is always uniform"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For an M/M/1 queue with ρ = 0.5 the mean number in system is","options":["0.5","1","2","0.25"],"answer":1,"explain":"L = ρ/(1 - ρ) = 1. 15. The degrees of freedom are k - 1.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The posterior of a Beta(a, b) prior after s successes and f failures is","options":["Beta(a, b)","Beta(a + s, b + f)","Beta(s, f)","Normal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Statistical significance means","options":["the effect is large","the result is unlikely under H0 at the chosen level","H0 is true","the sample is large"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
